const q = new JobQueue(2);
let active = 0, peak = 0, calls = 0;
const work = async () => {
  peak = Math.max(peak, ++active);
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 10));
  active--;
};
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) q.add(work);
const flaky = q.add(async () => { if (++calls < 3) throw new Error("fail"); return "ok"; });
const doomed = q.add(async () => { throw new Error("always"); }).catch((e) => e.message);

await q.onIdle();
console.assert(peak === 2 && calls === 3 && (await flaky) === "ok" && (await doomed) === "always");
```

Not run.

- Three retries means up to four attempts per job. Retries are immediate (no backoff) and keep the job's concurrency slot.
- `add()` rejects when a job fails for good, so attach a `.catch` or await it; an ignored rejection is an unhandled rejection, which kills Node by default.
- `onIdle()` resolves regardless of failures; per-job outcomes come from the `add()` promises.
